Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:46:22 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 39/46] gpiomem: the iCE40 SPI slave load through a Raspberry
 Pi's GPIO registers

A hardware trial on a Pi 3 Model B+ measured libgpiod at 0.440 MHz SPI_SCK,
under the iCE40's 1 MHz minimum for a slave SPI configuration (DS-02008
Table 4.24). GpiomemIce40Spi drives the same lines by writing the GPIO
registers of the BCM2835, BCM2836, BCM2837 and BCM2711 (Raspberry Pi 1 to
4) through /dev/gpiomem, one 4 KiB page mapped read/write:

  GPFSELn 0x00-0x14 (3 bits per pin, 000 input, 001 output), GPSET0 0x1C,
  GPCLR0 0x28, GPLEV0 0x34: BCM2835 ARM Peripherals section 6.1 and
  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 5.2. Volatile 32-bit accesses; GPIO 0-31
  only; the pull registers are never touched.

Selection is automatic (GpiomemIce40Spi::select): /proc/device-tree/
compatible naming brcm,bcm2835, 2836, 2837 or 2711 selects it, anything
else (a Pi 5's brcm,bcm2712, other hosts) and -d /dev/gpiochipN select
libgpiod, which still stops loud below 1 MHz. The choice and its reason are
logged. On a BCM283x host whose /dev/gpiomem is absent or cannot be opened
read/write the load stops, says why, asks "is the user in the gpio group?"
(and says what the user's groups and the device's mode are), and does not
fall back to libgpiod.

Each pin's function (its GPFSEL bits) is saved when the load starts and put
back, read back and checked, by restore_functions(): plain register
accesses on a fixed array, no allocation, async-signal-safe. The
destructor runs it on every way out of the load, the SIGINT, SIGTERM and
SIGHUP stop included. A pin whose function is an alternate one (SPI0's
GPIO8-11 for instance) is refused: /dev/gpiomem bypasses the kernel's pin
ownership, so a driver's pin is not taken over.

The clock is held inside the data sheet's range: before every write() the
loop is timed with zero masks (no pin moves) and a spin is calibrated, and
every SPI_SCK period is padded to 50 ns or more (20 MHz), every phase to
25 ns or more (tCCLKH, tCCLKL are 20 ns). Ice40's per-chunk and overall
1-25 MHz checks are unchanged and remain the authority, and the "SPI_SCK x
MHz" lines too. The register page is an Ice40GpioRegs, so the model test
runs the whole load against a model of the registers.

